==> CountSort
1. non-comparison algorithm
2. Range of number define (1-100) -->k
3.If range is not given then maximum number is range
4. No In-place algorithm
steps

1.find the maximum value from the arr and make a count array with max + 1 len.
2. In the count  the frequency of array
3. make this count array to  prefix sum if you want sorted in increasing order.
4. this prefix sum help you to find the last index of any number
5.traverse the array from back store value in output array
6.All output array to original arr.

Worst case: The largest element is much larger than the other elements.
O(n+k)
SPACE COMPLEXITY:
O(k)

==> RadixSort
1. non-comparison algorithm
2. digit by digit sorting , place value / position of digit in a number
in this algorithm we use the countSort method for each unit , tenth , hundred places

n = number of digits in the largest element
Best case: When all elements have the same number of digits.
O(a(n+b))

Average case: There are 'p' passes, and each digit can have up
to 'd' different values. O(p*(n+d))

Worst case: When all elements have the same number of digits except
one, which has a significantly larger
number of digits.
O(n2)

SPACE COMPLEXITY:
O(n+k)


===> BucketSort
this is use when the numbers are uniformly distributed
1. put all element in b no. of buckets.
2. Sort each bucket individually.
3.Take out all element and join them and merge them

TIME COMPLEXITY:
n = number of elements, k = number of buckets
Best case: When the elements are distributed in a balanced manner across the buckets.
O(n+k)
Average case: When the elements are distributed randomly in the list.
O(n)
Worst case: When every element is stored in the same bucket
O(n2)
SPACE COMPLEXITY:
O(n+k)
